WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.397 --> 00:00:03.672
- Keith and I are chefs, but not Korean.

00:00:03.672 --> 00:00:04.333
- Right.

00:00:04.333 --> 00:00:07.038
- Eugene is Korean, but not a chef.

00:00:07.038 --> 00:00:09.592
So, how is that going to do?

00:00:09.592 --> 00:00:10.590
- (splatter) Oh! (laughs)

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:11.658
- Zach!

00:00:11.658 --> 00:00:14.287
(upbeat music)

00:00:15.118 --> 00:00:18.751
- I think there is a growing
interest in Korean cuisine

00:00:18.751 --> 00:00:21.295
because the popular culture introduced it

00:00:21.295 --> 00:00:22.850
in such a delicious manner.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:23.790
- What do you do?

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:24.940
- I am Hyosun Ro.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:26.577
I am the Korean mom.

00:00:26.577 --> 00:00:29.433
- You're the Korean mom
we've always wanted to have.

00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:30.513
- Really?

00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:32.811
- Family members eating around the table,

00:00:32.811 --> 00:00:35.458
that's when many conflicts emerge.

00:00:35.458 --> 00:00:36.838
- Keith and Ned had mom, so Eugene's going

00:00:36.838 --> 00:00:37.710
to be my mom.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:38.790
- I'm not going to be your mom.

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:40.194
- So, I got mom here helping me out--

00:00:40.194 --> 00:00:41.089
- I am not your mom.

00:00:41.089 --> 00:00:42.970
- Consumption of food is
not just something that

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:45.570
your body needs, but it's something that,

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:46.998
also, your emotion needs.

00:00:46.998 --> 00:00:49.075
- Okay, today we're
going to make dukbokki.

00:00:49.075 --> 00:00:49.889
- Dick pokey.

00:00:49.889 --> 00:00:50.515
(Eugene laughs)

00:00:50.515 --> 00:00:53.174
- Dukbokki is a number
one street food in Korea.

00:00:53.174 --> 00:00:54.208
- Have you never had duck before?

00:00:54.208 --> 00:00:55.031
- This is duck?

00:00:55.031 --> 00:00:55.914
I've had duck before.

00:00:55.914 --> 00:00:57.969
- It's not the bird
duck, it's a rice cake.

00:00:57.969 --> 00:00:59.211
- That's a different kind of cake.

00:00:59.211 --> 00:01:01.138
- It tastes like string cheese
that rolled under the couch.

00:01:01.138 --> 00:01:02.508
So, mom, what's the first step?

00:01:02.508 --> 00:01:03.135
- Not your mom.

00:01:03.135 --> 00:01:06.397
- First thing we need to do
is to make anchovy broth.

00:01:06.397 --> 00:01:07.790
- Anchovy broth.

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:09.253
- Are you freaked out by little fish?

00:01:09.253 --> 00:01:10.391
- I'm not freaked out by it.

00:01:10.391 --> 00:01:11.053
- Put the whole thing in your mouth.

00:01:11.053 --> 00:01:12.097
(Zach mumbles)
There you go.

00:01:12.097 --> 00:01:12.551
- Ned?

00:01:12.551 --> 00:01:13.270
- Yeah?

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:14.129
- Do you want to do your job?

00:01:14.129 --> 00:01:14.954
- Sure.

00:01:14.954 --> 00:01:16.497
I didn't realize I had a job.

00:01:16.497 --> 00:01:18.553
Whee, see, see?

00:01:18.553 --> 00:01:19.330
- Just pour it in, Ned!

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:20.015
- Just pour it.

00:01:20.015 --> 00:01:21.350
- (in singsong voice) One
more cup of fish water,

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.555
one more cup of fish water.

00:01:23.555 --> 00:01:26.633
- Next thing we need to do is gochujang.

00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:28.036
- That smells good.

00:01:28.036 --> 00:01:29.733
- Gochujang?

00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:31.172
- Gochujang, you've heard of it, right?

00:01:31.172 --> 00:01:34.190
- I've seen it right
now, and it is very red.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:35.410
- Am I going to regret
how much I put on this?

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:36.186
Is it real spicy?

00:01:36.186 --> 00:01:37.093
- No, put on more.

00:01:37.093 --> 00:01:39.472
- If I drank that whole thing,
would I be in the hospital?

00:01:39.472 --> 00:01:40.251
- Stick that whole thing in your mouth.

00:01:40.251 --> 00:01:42.886
- You [censored] me hard.

00:01:42.886 --> 00:01:44.488
Oh my god, that was so spicy.

00:01:44.488 --> 00:01:45.614
- Three tablespoons.

00:01:45.614 --> 00:01:46.230
- (splatter) Oh!

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:47.471
- Zach!

00:01:47.471 --> 00:01:48.888
- Eugene's a mean daddy. (laughs)

00:01:48.888 --> 00:01:50.745
- You want to have extra spicy kick?

00:01:50.745 --> 00:01:51.732
- No.

00:01:51.732 --> 00:01:52.545
- Sure.

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:53.172
- Gochugaru.

00:01:53.172 --> 00:01:53.811
- I don't trust you--

00:01:53.811 --> 00:01:54.831
- Just put that whole thing in your mouth.

00:01:54.831 --> 00:01:55.853
- Eugene, it's red.

00:01:55.853 --> 00:01:57.433
- That's a spicy Tuesday.

00:01:57.433 --> 00:01:59.290
- This is one tablespoon soy sauce.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:00.589
- Go get it, bro.

00:02:00.589 --> 00:02:01.856
- Garlic.
- Whee.

00:02:01.856 --> 00:02:03.493
- Why do you have to try sugar?

00:02:03.493 --> 00:02:06.012
- Mmm, this looks red.

00:02:06.012 --> 00:02:07.857
- It's all about complexity and balance.
- All about balance.

00:02:07.857 --> 00:02:08.972
- Wow, complexity and balance.

00:02:08.972 --> 00:02:10.493
- I said balance at the same time as mom.

00:02:10.493 --> 00:02:11.631
- Can I put my duck in there?

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:12.850
- You can put your duck in there.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:14.777
- (singsong voice) All
the fingers in the pot,

00:02:14.777 --> 00:02:16.611
fingers in the pot.

00:02:16.611 --> 00:02:18.109
- I'm splishing splashing everywhere.

00:02:18.109 --> 00:02:19.315
- Yeah, don't splash.

00:02:19.315 --> 00:02:22.788
- This is called eomuk,
and it's fishy cake, and--

00:02:22.788 --> 00:02:24.030
(Keith and Ned chuckle)

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:27.594
- It's like a flattened, fried
fish cake of sorts. - Cool.

00:02:27.594 --> 00:02:29.950
- If you cut it diagonally...

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:31.112
- Yeah?

00:02:31.112 --> 00:02:32.075
- it will be pretty.

00:02:32.075 --> 00:02:33.492
- Ooh, go for it bro.
- Oh.

00:02:33.492 --> 00:02:34.187
- Go for it.

00:02:34.187 --> 00:02:35.535
- Little triangles, is that impressive?

00:02:35.535 --> 00:02:36.487
- I love triangles.

00:02:36.487 --> 00:02:37.427
- I'd chop the rest of this up.

00:02:37.427 --> 00:02:40.015
- Okay. Oh no! (laughs)

00:02:40.015 --> 00:02:40.527
Should I throw it?

00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:41.711
- No, you're not throwing the fish cake.

00:02:41.711 --> 00:02:42.754
- It seems like a fun thing to throw.

00:02:44.544 --> 00:02:45.879
(laughs)

00:02:45.879 --> 00:02:46.959
- I love cabbage.

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:47.585
- And what do I do with that?

00:02:47.585 --> 00:02:48.606
Just chop it up?

00:02:48.606 --> 00:02:49.825
- [Ned] Nice knife skills, Keith.

00:02:49.825 --> 00:02:50.941
- [Keith] Thanks.

00:02:50.941 --> 00:02:54.098
- So, can we put in the cabbage yet?

00:02:54.098 --> 00:02:54.981
- Sure.

00:02:54.981 --> 00:02:56.442
- Great! Go for it, Ned.

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:58.162
Just grab a fistful.

00:02:58.162 --> 00:02:59.926
So, once you've got everything boiled

00:02:59.926 --> 00:03:02.504
in for a second, then
you add these fish cakes.

00:03:02.504 --> 00:03:03.839
- Now we just let this simmer for awhile.

00:03:03.839 --> 00:03:05.161
- Dude, we're a great team.

00:03:05.161 --> 00:03:07.124
There is no flaws in our system,

00:03:07.124 --> 00:03:08.378
we worked really well together--

00:03:08.378 --> 00:03:09.620
- No fish cakes were lost.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.245
- Sesame oil is very important.

00:03:11.245 --> 00:03:13.416
- Oh, can I taste some?

00:03:13.416 --> 00:03:14.357
- It's going to taste like oil.

00:03:14.357 --> 00:03:15.366
- Oh my god, that's delicious.

00:03:15.366 --> 00:03:16.481
It's probably good for the skin, too.

00:03:16.481 --> 00:03:17.340
- All right.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.186
- Oh no, oh no!
- Ned, no.

00:03:19.186 --> 00:03:20.963
- They say it's good for the skin.

00:03:20.963 --> 00:03:22.240
- Ned, you said that.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.224
Ned, no! Mom!

00:03:24.224 --> 00:03:27.986
Ned, let me stand near the table. No!

00:03:27.986 --> 00:03:31.539
- So it's now time to put
our final ingredients.

00:03:31.539 --> 00:03:32.618
- Oh, we can all do it together.

00:03:32.618 --> 00:03:34.964
- Sprinkle some scallions.
- Yay.

00:03:34.964 --> 00:03:38.679
- Yes.
- Sprinkle.

00:03:38.679 --> 00:03:42.265
- Oh no, it was all in one spot.

00:03:42.265 --> 00:03:43.578
- That was a group [censored] up.

00:03:43.578 --> 00:03:44.703
- You're not a part of this family.

00:03:45.843 --> 00:03:47.779
- Nice, time for tasting.

00:03:47.779 --> 00:03:49.010
- Okay.
- Mmm.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.044
- Oh my goodness.

00:03:52.044 --> 00:03:54.110
- It's good but it's spicy as [censored].

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:55.585
- It's so well balanced.

00:03:55.585 --> 00:03:58.626
- We're all having a Korean good time.

00:03:58.626 --> 00:03:59.543
- You good, Keith?

00:03:59.543 --> 00:04:00.623
- Yeah, I'm good.
(Zach laughs)

00:04:00.623 --> 00:04:01.041
- You need water?

00:04:01.041 --> 00:04:01.968
- Probably.

00:04:01.968 --> 00:04:03.967
- Could we get water?
- I don't know.

00:04:03.967 --> 00:04:05.743
- Alright mom, what's our next dish?

00:04:05.743 --> 00:04:07.287
- So, we're about to cook one of my

00:04:07.287 --> 00:04:09.284
favorite childhood Korean dishes.

00:04:09.284 --> 00:04:11.849
- Mhmm. Korean kimbap
is very well seasoned.

00:04:11.849 --> 00:04:13.463
It's very easy to make.

00:04:13.463 --> 00:04:16.563
It only takes three ingredients inside.

00:04:16.563 --> 00:04:18.269
Because we're making mini kimbap,

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:19.708
we're going to cut this in half.

00:04:19.708 --> 00:04:21.677
- What's over there?

00:04:22.088 --> 00:04:23.099
- Eugene!

00:04:23.099 --> 00:04:25.305
- Oh my gosh, to my
core I just got chills.

00:04:25.305 --> 00:04:27.637
That's the way my mom says "Eugene!",

00:04:27.637 --> 00:04:28.868
and she'd get the rice scooper.

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:30.285
She would just be like
(makes slapping sounds).

00:04:30.285 --> 00:04:31.527
- Oh, no, no! (laughs)

00:04:31.527 --> 00:04:32.908
- You have to season this really well

00:04:32.908 --> 00:04:34.267
for kimbap to taste good.

00:04:34.267 --> 00:04:35.544
We're ready to roll.

00:04:35.544 --> 00:04:40.002
Lift the end that's closer
to you, and just roll it.

00:04:40.002 --> 00:04:41.964
There's no really magic to it.

00:04:41.964 --> 00:04:43.706
- The other Try Guys have
to do this on their own,

00:04:43.706 --> 00:04:44.866
and they're going to compete to see

00:04:44.866 --> 00:04:47.038
who can make the best mini kimbap.

00:04:47.038 --> 00:04:48.116
I think Keith's will taste the best.

00:04:48.116 --> 00:04:49.418
I think Ned's will look the best.

00:04:49.418 --> 00:04:50.357
- Ned all the way.

00:04:50.357 --> 00:04:51.239
- What are you sawing?

00:04:51.239 --> 00:04:52.226
- I think Zach's going to be the one

00:04:52.226 --> 00:04:55.060
that's just going to be an
explosion of rice and stuff.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:58.159
- Okay, so we each have
two minutes to make kimbap.

00:04:58.159 --> 00:04:59.123
- If you've been watching this video,

00:04:59.123 --> 00:05:00.806
you probably expect me
to do horribly on this.

00:05:00.806 --> 00:05:02.118
I'm going to try and prove you wrong.

00:05:02.118 --> 00:05:04.939
- First, rice, already seasoned.

00:05:04.939 --> 00:05:06.367
I've got a nice bed of rice.

00:05:06.367 --> 00:05:07.586
- You don't need that much rice.

00:05:07.586 --> 00:05:08.119
- Take your time.

00:05:08.119 --> 00:05:10.384
Patience is important when cooking.

00:05:10.384 --> 00:05:11.940
- How many of these did she put in?

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:12.602
Two.

00:05:12.602 --> 00:05:13.542
I hope it was two.

00:05:13.542 --> 00:05:15.643
- He was wrong, you only
need one of these things.

00:05:15.643 --> 00:05:19.405
- I rolled things in college, so I have

00:05:19.405 --> 00:05:21.541
prior knowledge on how to do this.

00:05:21.541 --> 00:05:23.259
- I think I might have
put too much rice in here.

00:05:23.259 --> 00:05:25.337
- And you roll it, and
you're [censored] done.

00:05:25.337 --> 00:05:26.382
This is not that hard.

00:05:26.382 --> 00:05:28.065
- Oh no, I'm losing time.

00:05:28.065 --> 00:05:30.062
So hard to recover once
you've [censored] it up.

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:30.967
- Lube this up.

00:05:30.967 --> 00:05:31.978
- Sprinkle dinkle.

00:05:31.978 --> 00:05:33.197
- This is real slippery.

00:05:33.197 --> 00:05:34.124
- Let's make another.

00:05:34.124 --> 00:05:36.145
I'm not feeding my only
one I made, Eugene.

00:05:36.145 --> 00:05:37.318
[Censored] Eugene.

00:05:37.318 --> 00:05:38.537
(laughs)

00:05:41.727 --> 00:05:43.053
(long beep)

00:05:43.053 --> 00:05:43.691
- Yes!

00:05:43.691 --> 00:05:46.106
- So now we're going to
judge the other Try Guys'

00:05:46.106 --> 00:05:47.906
kimbap to see which one is the best.

00:05:47.906 --> 00:05:50.111
- This one is not the best.

00:05:50.111 --> 00:05:52.363
- It's a very, like, (muffled)
person in a very small shirt.

00:05:52.363 --> 00:05:52.828
- Yeah.

00:05:52.828 --> 00:05:54.523
- What about the sesame on each one?

00:05:54.523 --> 00:05:56.090
- I think this was a little bit overdone.

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:57.844
- Looks like there's a lot of seeds.

00:05:57.844 --> 00:06:01.512
But the extras on the sides here

00:06:01.512 --> 00:06:02.927
is very Korean, actually.

00:06:02.927 --> 00:06:04.762
- It's very Korean, right.

00:06:04.762 --> 00:06:05.146
- You should probably eat it

00:06:05.146 --> 00:06:06.631
just to be sure which
one tastes good, right?

00:06:06.631 --> 00:06:08.570
- So your official decision is?

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:09.952
- The middle one.
(Zach yells)

00:06:09.952 --> 00:06:10.626
- Number two!

00:06:10.626 --> 00:06:14.004
- My name is Keith, and I
know how to cook, y'all.

00:06:14.004 --> 00:06:15.502
- Who is second best?

00:06:15.502 --> 00:06:16.744
- This one, yeah.
- Who is--

00:06:16.744 --> 00:06:20.388
- Yeah, I'm the second best
Korean cook of all time, yay.

00:06:20.388 --> 00:06:23.245
- This carrot sticking out
is a feature, not a bug, huh?

00:06:23.245 --> 00:06:24.429
- No, we Koreans like it.

00:06:24.429 --> 00:06:26.322
- We've all learned something today.

00:06:26.322 --> 00:06:31.322
- (in unison) Cheers and
thanks to our Korean mom.

00:06:31.441 --> 00:06:33.565
- Thank you, you're welcome.

00:06:33.565 --> 00:06:35.621
Thank you, I love you too.

00:06:35.621 --> 00:06:38.523
Korean food is not that hard to make.

00:06:38.523 --> 00:06:40.102
It's very approachable.

00:06:40.102 --> 00:06:42.888
Select a dish, find a
good recipe, and try it.

00:06:42.888 --> 00:06:45.223
The more you practice, the better you get.

00:06:45.223 --> 00:06:47.508
- (in unison) Try Mom.

